FLASHCARD 0
0 Script Skeleton, Strict Mode & Traps

Standard script header & strict mode:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    IFS=$'\n\t'

What strict flags do:
    set -e          - exit immediately if a command exits non-zero
    set -u          - treat unset variables as an error
    set -o pipefail - pipeline fails if any command fails (not just the last)
    IFS=$'\n\t'     - safer word splitting (avoids space surprises)

Cleanup & signal traps:
    trap 'rm -f "$tmpfile"' EXIT
    trap 'echo "Interrupted"; exit 130' INT TERM

FLASHCARD 1
1 Variables, Quoting & Parameter Expansion

Quoting rules:
    "$var"          - always quote variables to prevent word splitting & globbing
    '$var'          - literal string (no variable or command expansion)
    $(cmd)          - command substitution (preferred over backticks `cmd`)

Parameter expansion:
    ${var:-default} - use 'default' if var is unset or empty
    ${var:=default} - assign 'default' to var if unset or empty
    ${var:?err msg} - exit with error message if var is unset/empty
    ${#var}         - string length (or array element count for ${#arr[@]})
    ${path##*/}     - basename (strip longest prefix up to last /)
    ${path%/*}      - dirname (strip shortest suffix from last /)
    ${str/old/new}  - replace first match; ${str//old/new} replaces all

FLASHCARD 2
2 Conditionals, Tests & Arithmetic

Test expressions:
    [[ -z "$s" ]]           - string is empty; [[ -n "$s" ]] is non-empty
    [[ "$a" == "$b" ]]      - string equality (supports glob/pattern on RHS)
    [[ "$a" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]  - regex match against BASH_REMATCH
    [[ -f "$f" && -r "$f" ]] - regular file exists and is readable
    [[ -d "$d" ]]           - directory exists
    [[ "$n" -gt 10 ]]       - integer comparison (-eq, -ne, -lt, -le, -gt, -ge)

Arithmetic evaluation:
    (( count++ ))           - arithmetic context (no $ needed inside (( )) )
    sum=$(( a + b * 2 ))    - arithmetic expansion producing a value

FLASHCARD 3
3 Loops, Functions & Positional Parameters

Loops:
    for item in "$@"; do ... done          - iterate cleanly over arguments
    for f in *.log; do ... done            - globbing (nullglob avoids literal *.log)
    while IFS= read -r line; do ... done < file  - safe line-by-line file reading
    while true; do sleep 5; done           - poll/watch loop

Functions & arguments:
    my_func() { local x="$1"; return 0; }  - 'local' scopes variables to function
    $# / $@ / $*                           - arg count / all args as separate words / single string

FLASHCARD 4
4 Arrays & Associative Arrays (Dictionaries)

Indexed arrays:
    arr=("one" "two" "three")
    arr+=("four")                          - append element
    echo "${arr[0]}"                       - first element
    echo "${arr[@]}"                       - all elements (quoted properly)
    echo "${#arr[@]}"                      - array length

Associative arrays (Bash 4+):
    declare -A map=( ["host"]="db1" ["port"]="5432" )
    map["env"]="prod"
    echo "${map[host]}"
    echo "${!map[@]}"                      - all keys

FLASHCARD 5
5 Redirections, Pipelines & Process Substitution

File descriptors & redirections:
    cmd > out.log 2>&1     - redirect stdout and stderr to same file
    cmd &> all.log         - Bash shorthand for stdout + stderr to file
    cmd 2>/dev/null        - silence error messages
    cmd <<'EOF'            - quoted heredoc (no variable expansion inside)

Process substitution & grouping:
    diff <(sort a.txt) <(sort b.txt)  - feed command outputs as files
    { echo "Header"; cat body.txt; } > report.txt  - group commands in current shell
    (cd /tmp && ls)        - run in subshell without changing caller's directory

FLASHCARD 6
6 Practical Bash One-Liners: Files & Text

Batch rename extension (.jpeg -> .jpg):
    for f in *.jpeg; do [[ -e "$f" ]] && mv -- "$f" "${f%.jpeg}.jpg"; done

Find and delete empty files in current tree:
    find . -type f -empty -delete

Print line numbers with non-empty lines:
    grep -n '[^[:space:]]' file.txt

Extract unique sorted values from column 2 of a CSV/TSV:
    awk -F',' 'NR>1 {print $2}' data.csv | sort -u

FLASHCARD 7
7 Practical Bash One-Liners: Safety, Loops & Quick Ops

Backup a file with a timestamp:
    cp -a config.yml "config.yml.$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S).bak"

Wait until a TCP port is listening:
    until timeout 1 bash -c '</dev/tcp/127.0.0.1/8080' 2>/dev/null; do sleep 0.5; done

Run a command across directories safely:
    for d in */; do (cd "$d" && git status -s); done

Dry-run / preview destructive rm with find:
    find /tmp/old_logs -type f -mtime +14 -print

